WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:03.580
So in this part of the course, we're

2
00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:05.839
going to be looking at the introduction to Lumen,

3
00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:09.179
where we're going to break down what it is and how it works.

4
00:00:09.179 --> 00:00:11.689
So let's get started.

5
00:00:11.689 --> 00:00:14.169
First up, many will ask, what is Lumen?

6
00:00:14.169 --> 00:00:18.410
So Lumen is Unreal Engine 5's fully dynamic global

7
00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.769
illumination and reflection system.

8
00:00:20.769 --> 00:00:23.690
It's designed for next generation consoles, default

9
00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.609
global illumination, and reflection system.

10
00:00:25.609 --> 00:00:27.170
Now, keep in mind, it doesn't

11
00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:28.850
have to be just for games.

12
00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:31.769
This can also be done for Final Pixel,

13
00:00:31.809 --> 00:00:37.210
as well as VR training or simulation.

14
00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.689
So it works pretty well now and works really great

15
00:00:39.689 --> 00:00:42.640
with those venues.

16
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.479
Not dependent, unlike ray tracing,

17
00:00:44.479 --> 00:00:47.700
on having the latest machine, but higher settings

18
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:48.840
are GPU expensive.

19
00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:50.600
So you have to keep those things in mind.

20
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.320
And I'll point those out later on in the course.

21
00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.179
Lumen renders diffuse interreflection

22
00:00:56.179 --> 00:00:59.320
with infinite bounces and indirect specular reflections

23
00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.560
in large, detailed environments.

24
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.560
At scales ranging from millimeters to kilometers.

25
00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:05.900
So it's really efficient.

26
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.680
It really tries to help you get the best look

27
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:09.719
that you can.

28
00:01:09.719 --> 00:01:11.840
And I'm going to walk you through some of the tools

29
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:14.599
and how to get things in the right direction.

30
00:01:14.599 --> 00:01:16.439
So also, what is it?

31
00:01:16.439 --> 00:01:19.079
Now, many people will ask, how does this

32
00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:21.040
work compared to the old versions of Unreal,

33
00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:22.920
if you are used to or understand

34
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.920
the old versions of Unreal, where you would bake your light.

35
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.760
But in Lumen, we're getting an update on the fly.

36
00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.560
We're getting our global illumination

37
00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.640
and the photon calculations back at the camera being sent back

38
00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.000
there, so that we're getting a nice, clean, updated,

39
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.959
realistic scene.

40
00:01:40.959 --> 00:01:47.159
So diffuse indirect lighting, Lumen GI, light bounces

41
00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:50.560
and color bleeds is what it represents.

42
00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:55.599
So Lumen also provides infinite diffuse bounces overall.

43
00:01:55.599 --> 00:01:58.680
Unreal Engine 5's Nanite visualized geometry

44
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.760
allows geometry to be much more detailed.

45
00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.439
And Lumen achieves a full resolution shading

46
00:02:05.439 --> 00:02:09.400
while computing indirect lighting cheaper than before.

47
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.569
So it actually is really efficient.

48
00:02:12.569 --> 00:02:16.289
Sky lighting is solved as part of Lumen's final gather

49
00:02:16.289 --> 00:02:17.250
process.

50
00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:20.969
It includes sky shadowing, allows indoor space

51
00:02:20.969 --> 00:02:23.169
to be much darker than outdoor lighting,

52
00:02:23.169 --> 00:02:26.770
and have much more of a natural effect.

53
00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:30.090
Lumen also provides a lower quality global illumination

54
00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:33.409
for lit translucency and volumetric fog.

55
00:02:33.409 --> 00:02:36.129
So we've really thought about how to optimize this and get

56
00:02:36.129 --> 00:02:40.729
it the best possible tool for your lighting needs.

57
00:02:40.729 --> 00:02:44.180
And it works really, really great.

58
00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:47.099
Lumen emissive materials, though, can be expensive.

59
00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:49.259
And we're going to go and take a look at Unreal.

60
00:02:49.259 --> 00:02:51.340
I'm going to point out a few things with that,

61
00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.419
as well as we're going to look at the settings

62
00:02:53.419 --> 00:02:56.139
that you have at your fingertips in the project settings

63
00:02:56.139 --> 00:02:58.889
and in the post-process volume.

64
00:02:58.889 --> 00:03:01.009
You see, emissive materials propagate light

65
00:03:01.009 --> 00:03:03.969
through Lumen's final gather with no additional performance

66
00:03:03.969 --> 00:03:04.689
cost.

67
00:03:04.689 --> 00:03:06.009
It's pretty awesome.

68
00:03:06.009 --> 00:03:07.930
But you can run into trouble when

69
00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:11.210
you have small pieces of geometry that you want to glow.

70
00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:12.729
There may be noise, and it may not

71
00:03:12.729 --> 00:03:14.729
be accurately represented.

72
00:03:14.729 --> 00:03:15.889
So there's a limit.

73
00:03:15.889 --> 00:03:18.169
So you have to keep things in mind.

74
00:03:18.169 --> 00:03:19.490
And you can see the note here.

75
00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:21.889
It says, however, there's a limit to how small.

76
00:03:21.889 --> 00:03:26.250
So again, emphasizing that, be careful as you do that.

77
00:03:26.289 --> 00:03:28.810
You might want to actually have a physical light attached

78
00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:31.370
to an object, or maybe even have

79
00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:34.449
some sort of pre-baked system in there and brought in

80
00:03:34.449 --> 00:03:37.449
and lighting things via vertex and via texture

81
00:03:37.449 --> 00:03:41.110
if they really are small and really need to be accurate.

82
00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:43.789
But for the most part, you can use Lumen

83
00:03:43.789 --> 00:03:45.389
when it comes to emissive materials,

84
00:03:45.389 --> 00:03:47.810
as long as you keep those things in mind.

85
00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:51.210
So Lumen solves indirect specular or reflections

86
00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.810
for full range of material roughness values.

87
00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:55.250
And it works really well.

88
00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:57.330
Now, you might get noise every once in a while dealing

89
00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.409
with your roughness and your materials.

90
00:03:59.409 --> 00:04:01.930
That is always something to keep in mind

91
00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:04.860
when it comes to updating GI on the fly.

92
00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.900
But you have several CVARs, and you

93
00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:08.500
can see in some of our documentation,

94
00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.340
that will help clean up some of that.

95
00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:12.340
And I'm going to point you to some of the CVARs

96
00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:13.860
that you can work with.

97
00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.459
Diffused GI and shadowed skylight

98
00:04:16.459 --> 00:04:18.420
can be seen in all reflections.

99
00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.740
And Lumen reflections also support clear coat materials,

100
00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:24.500
which is a really added plus, because then you're

101
00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.139
getting your updates on the fly.

102
00:04:26.139 --> 00:04:27.779
And you can control your materials

103
00:04:27.779 --> 00:04:30.370
and make things work really great.

104
00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:34.240
So what does it replace working with Lumen?

105
00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:37.480
It replaces skylight with AO or unshadowed.

106
00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:40.439
It replaces screen space global illumination.

107
00:04:40.439 --> 00:04:44.639
It replaces ray tracing GI and third party GI solutions,

108
00:04:44.639 --> 00:04:48.600
such as NVIDIA's RTXGI.

109
00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.439
So let's talk about how to set it up.

110
00:04:50.439 --> 00:04:53.079
And I'll also point out about getting things

111
00:04:53.079 --> 00:04:57.910
to be emissive in your scene.

112
00:04:57.910 --> 00:04:59.870
So we have a scene here that has Lumen.

113
00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:01.910
This is the one that we used earlier.

114
00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:04.629
But let's talk about how to set up a scene.

115
00:05:04.629 --> 00:05:06.250
Now, we have project settings here.

116
00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:08.709
This is where we're going to initially set things up and get

117
00:05:08.709 --> 00:05:10.889
it in the right direction.

118
00:05:10.889 --> 00:05:13.389
So I'm going to open this up.

119
00:05:13.389 --> 00:05:16.029
In your project settings, you want to scroll down and go

120
00:05:16.029 --> 00:05:18.339
to Rendering.

121
00:05:18.339 --> 00:05:21.019
In Rendering, we're going to scroll down a bit more.

122
00:05:21.019 --> 00:05:22.600
And we're going to make sure that we

123
00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:25.980
have a dynamic global illumination method set to Lumen.

124
00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:27.660
Now, it should be on by default.

125
00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.139
But there are a few things you do need to edit.

126
00:05:30.139 --> 00:05:32.620
And also, if you're grabbing something from the marketplace,

127
00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:35.139
I'm going to point out some things to keep in mind.

128
00:05:35.139 --> 00:05:38.060
You'll notice a reflection method right now is Lumen.

129
00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:39.740
Now, in the post-process volume,

130
00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:42.180
you can change some of these if you need.

131
00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:43.819
And I'll point those out later.

132
00:05:43.819 --> 00:05:46.379
But for now, we're going to keep things all Lumen.

133
00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:49.379
We have a ray lighting mode here.

134
00:05:49.379 --> 00:05:52.860
And you'll see that it says Hit Reflection or Surface Catch.

135
00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:54.699
Hit gives you a bit more accuracy.

136
00:05:54.819 --> 00:05:58.339
And Surface Catch is a little bit lower in quality.

137
00:05:58.339 --> 00:06:00.740
So you have to keep those things in mind, what you want

138
00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:04.500
and what kind of hardware you're working with,

139
00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:06.560
as well as what you have in your scene.

140
00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:08.180
You'll notice our shadows are always going

141
00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:10.100
to be virtual shadow maps.

142
00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:13.899
That is what works with Lumen and is the ideal setting

143
00:06:13.899 --> 00:06:16.279
to set.

144
00:06:16.279 --> 00:06:18.879
You'll notice, too, our software ray tracing mode.

145
00:06:18.879 --> 00:06:21.639
We can do detail tracing or global tracing.

146
00:06:21.639 --> 00:06:23.519
One, obviously, is cheaper than the other.

147
00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:26.449
Detail tracing is a lot higher than global tracing.

148
00:06:26.449 --> 00:06:28.990
And you can choose that based, again, on your scene

149
00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:30.490
and what you're working with under the hood

150
00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:32.660
in your computer.

151
00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:35.240
You'll notice also we have Use Hardware Ray Tracing

152
00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:36.240
when available.

153
00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:39.019
This is important to turn on if you have the ability

154
00:06:39.019 --> 00:06:41.480
to be able to run that.

155
00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:44.279
You also want to make sure under Hardware Ray Tracing,

156
00:06:44.279 --> 00:06:46.519
you have Supports Hardware Ray Tracing.

157
00:06:46.519 --> 00:06:48.500
Also, this needs to be turned on

158
00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.279
if you have it available.

159
00:06:50.279 --> 00:06:51.740
It is very helpful to keep these on

160
00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:53.420
because they will then support,

161
00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:55.060
as you're working with Lumen,

162
00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:58.209
and get things to calculate and update on the fly

163
00:06:58.209 --> 00:07:01.259
and give you the best possible results.

164
00:07:01.259 --> 00:07:04.519
Now, you'll hear me mention this again in the class,

165
00:07:04.519 --> 00:07:06.459
but I'll emphasize this now,

166
00:07:06.459 --> 00:07:08.399
and I'll repeat myself later.

167
00:07:08.399 --> 00:07:12.480
When it comes to Lumen, Nanite, and virtual shadow maps,

168
00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:14.240
they're like the three musketeers.

169
00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:16.439
They really were made to work together.

170
00:07:16.439 --> 00:07:19.540
So it's very important when you are building your scene

171
00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:21.639
to not rule out working with Nanite

172
00:07:21.639 --> 00:07:24.879
because it'll give you the best, most realistic geometry,

173
00:07:24.879 --> 00:07:27.259
but also virtual shadow maps will complement

174
00:07:27.259 --> 00:07:29.160
that realistic geometry to give you

175
00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:32.069
the best possible results for your shadows.

176
00:07:32.069 --> 00:07:33.350
And then Lumen will give you

177
00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:35.990
the best possible results for your lighting.

178
00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:38.660
All those work really great together.

179
00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:40.300
Now, we talked about emissive.

180
00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:41.120
If you go over here,

181
00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:44.000
you'll see there's some emissive lights.

182
00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:45.379
These are pretty decent size,

183
00:07:45.379 --> 00:07:48.180
so this isn't going to be too much of a trouble.

184
00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:50.899
But if I had a smaller piece of geometry

185
00:07:50.939 --> 00:07:52.019
and I want to make it glow,

186
00:07:52.019 --> 00:07:54.420
the accuracy might be an issue.

187
00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:57.259
You'll notice we are getting a little bit of noise here.

188
00:07:57.259 --> 00:07:59.779
You'll notice from our point light here,

189
00:07:59.779 --> 00:08:02.220
we're not getting much noise, it's pretty clean.

190
00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:03.879
But over here with this emissive material,

191
00:08:03.879 --> 00:08:06.519
we may be getting a bit of noise.

192
00:08:06.519 --> 00:08:08.540
So what you can do in that case,

193
00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:11.160
you'll notice even if I increase the size,

194
00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:15.420
the noise may persist, not necessarily go away.

195
00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:18.040
There are CVARs to be able to clean some of that up.

196
00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:20.720
And I can also go into the material itself

197
00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:24.600
and decide that I want to edit some things such as roughness

198
00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:25.920
and a few other items.

199
00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:29.079
And I'm going to repeat myself later in the course.

200
00:08:29.079 --> 00:08:31.120
So as you lower your roughness,

201
00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:34.029
you can see some of the noise goes away

202
00:08:34.029 --> 00:08:34.950
as you keep it clear.

203
00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:38.450
But as you increase the roughness like it does with,

204
00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:40.870
like what happens with ray tracing,

205
00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:43.029
you can end up getting a bit more noise.

206
00:08:43.029 --> 00:08:44.789
You can also flatten it out

207
00:08:44.789 --> 00:08:48.029
and make it so there's even no noise whatsoever.

208
00:08:48.029 --> 00:08:50.009
But as soon as you hit that middle ground,

209
00:08:50.009 --> 00:08:52.409
just keep things in mind with your materials,

210
00:08:52.409 --> 00:08:55.009
how you want that material to be represented

211
00:08:55.009 --> 00:09:00.220
because noise may try to creep in.

212
00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:01.299
You also want to make sure

213
00:09:01.299 --> 00:09:03.620
that you're using Shader Model 6.

214
00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:05.460
Now this is more important

215
00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:07.860
if you're getting stuff off the marketplace,

216
00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:11.000
because by default Shader Model 6 should be on.

217
00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:12.539
If you're not sure how to get there,

218
00:09:12.539 --> 00:09:16.100
let me show you how to get there in Unreal.

219
00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:19.539
In Unreal, you go to your project settings,

220
00:09:19.539 --> 00:09:24.779
you can simply type in SM6,

221
00:09:24.779 --> 00:09:28.259
and you'll see that Shader Model 6 is ready to go.

222
00:09:28.259 --> 00:09:31.259
Now I'm going to turn mine on and it's going to reboot.

223
00:09:31.259 --> 00:09:33.419
And when it does, that's going to actually give me

224
00:09:33.419 --> 00:09:37.179
the best possible results for my virtual shadow maps,

225
00:09:37.179 --> 00:09:39.500
as well as going to make it so that my visualizers

226
00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:42.740
for Lumen, Nanite, and virtual shadow maps

227
00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:44.480
are working correctly.

228
00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:46.950
So let's go ahead and turn that on.

229
00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:50.039
So I've turned that on and it's restarted.

230
00:09:50.039 --> 00:09:51.840
Awesome, that's great.

231
00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:54.220
So we got things working in the right direction

232
00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:56.120
with our Lumen scene.

233
00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:58.000
Now we talked about in the project settings,

234
00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.100
how we have to make sure Lumen is on,

235
00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:02.179
that we can control a lighting mode,

236
00:10:02.179 --> 00:10:04.240
software ray tracing mode,

237
00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:07.039
and we also make sure that things are supported

238
00:10:07.039 --> 00:10:09.610
for how we're ray tracing.

239
00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:13.110
You'll notice in here that you also can,

240
00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:15.629
and you'll notice, what I mean is you can notice

241
00:10:15.629 --> 00:10:18.750
in my notes that it says in the post process volume,

242
00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:20.830
you can edit some of these settings,

243
00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:23.399
which is really great.

244
00:10:23.399 --> 00:10:25.460
And again, also by default,

245
00:10:25.460 --> 00:10:28.600
your generate mesh distance fields will be on.

246
00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:29.919
Now again, if you're grabbing something

247
00:10:29.919 --> 00:10:32.200
from the marketplace, you want to step through

248
00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:34.200
and make sure these things are on.

249
00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:36.220
Let me show you.

250
00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:40.799
So in my project settings, I'm going to pop that up.

251
00:10:40.799 --> 00:10:44.070
In here, I'm going to type in

252
00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:46.759
generate mesh distance fields.

253
00:10:46.759 --> 00:10:48.080
And you want to make sure that's on,

254
00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:51.519
especially if you're getting things from the marketplace.

255
00:10:51.559 --> 00:10:52.899
They may be a little bit older

256
00:10:52.899 --> 00:10:54.600
and may not have these things on.

257
00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:57.120
But by default, working with a Lumen scene,

258
00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:59.419
these should be active.

259
00:10:59.419 --> 00:11:03.480
We also want to make sure for better AO built into Lumen,

260
00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:06.519
we want to make sure we are not using the old default.

